### 3c. Why 0 issues were closed

Closing on the evidence above would mean asserting "the artifact in R2 no longer crashes"
from a repro that is not that artifact. The committed seed reproduces the *signature*; the
July reports may or may not be the same input. Section 3a's stale-build hypothesis is
strong, but it is a hypothesis. The honest state is: **17 issues have a live, proven,
CI-enforced gate on their signature as of this branch, and 0 have been verified against
their own repro.** Whoever can reach `s3://zenfuzz` should re-run those 17 artifacts against
`main`; that is a five-minute job with credentials and impossible without them.

### 3d. #516 is different, and it is NOT stale-line

`#516` (2026-08-12, `parse_seq_header`, x86_64, `panic rav1d-safe/src/safe_simd/mc.rs:5337:56`).
At `d26c404`, the `main` of that date, line 5337 is:

    let a10 = loadu_128!(<&[u16; 8]>::try_from(&src[col + 10..col + 18]).unwrap());

Column 56 lands on that `try_from(...).unwrap()`. This is a real panicking construct in
current code — a `TryFromSliceError` unwrap when the 16bpc horizontal-filter source window
runs short — so unlike the 17 above, #516's signature is consistent with the source it was
reported against. **Treat it as live.** It is an x86 path (`mc.rs`, not `mc_arm.rs`) and
cannot be exercised on this aarch64 box; it was not reproduced here.

The parent brief asked whether "a panic in motion compensation reached from a
*sequence-header* target" means the target decodes further than its name suggests, or the
signature is misattributed. **The former.** `fuzz/fuzz_targets/parse_seq_header.rs` is a
full `decoder.decode(data)` + `flush()`; it differs from `decode_obu` only in
`threads = 1`, `inloop_filters = none()` and an explicit `decode_frame_type = All`. Nothing
stops at the sequence header. Its doc comment ("focuses on parser correctness rather than
full reconstruction") describes an intent the code does not implement. That also explains
why the farm files one panic site under two or three different target names. Worth renaming
the target, or actually restricting it — not done here.


