## 5. The liveness gate is aarch64-only — learned from CI, recorded here

The first push wired the `__ablate` liveness leg to two CI legs, x86_64 Linux and
native arm64. **The x86_64 leg failed**, and the cause is worth keeping because it is
the same shape as the vacuity this whole branch is about: an assertion that could
never be satisfied, rather than one that was not met.

    $ rg -n 'ablate::note\(' src/ | sed 's/:.*//' | sort -u
    src/safe_simd/cdef_arm.rs
    src/safe_simd/itx_arm.rs
    src/safe_simd/looprestoration_arm.rs

Every `note(Family::..)` call site is in an `*_arm.rs` module. On x86_64,
`--features __ablate` compiles the counters in and nothing increments them: the
corpus does reach the kernels, the instrument simply is not wired to them on that
target. `ablate::ENABLED` answers "is the feature on", not "does this build have call
sites", and the test was reading the first as if it were the second.

The `units > 0` assertion is now `cfg!(target_arch = "aarch64")`-gated, the test
reports which of the two non-instrumented cases it is in, and CI runs the leg on
`ubuntu-24.04-arm` only. The crash gate itself
(`fuzz_regression_seeds_do_not_panic`) is unconditional and passed on `ubuntu-latest`
in that same failing run.

Consequence for anyone extending this: `md5_inventory --activity` and every other
consumer of these counters carries the same arch restriction. AGENT_BRIEF's note that
only itx, cdef and looprestoration are instrumented is true on aarch64 and an
overstatement everywhere else — on x86_64, none of the nine are.
